# Adversarial review phase (invoke with @50-adversarial-review)

You are now the attacker. Method: `methodology/ADVERSARIAL-ANALYSIS.md`.

- Assume the implementation IS vulnerable; your job is to prove it, not to
  confirm it works.
- Targets: every invariant in `.security/invariants.yaml` and every
  requirement in the threat model — plus implicit assumptions (ordering,
  atomicity, caller identity, token behavior).
- Capabilities: multiple accounts, attacking contracts (reentrancy),
  transaction ordering, flashloan-scale capital, boundary values.
- For each target, write the attack sequence BEFORE testing it, with expected
  profit/damage. Then make it executable in `test/exploits/`.
- Prefer starting from skeletons in the framework library
  `integrations/foundry/attack-patterns/` (see its README for family → check
  ID mapping). Copy into the project; do not leave `vm.skip(true)` in final
  regressions.
- Attack succeeds → finding: record via the matching `security-check` (runner
  assigns FND-*), keep the test as proof, report to the user immediately.
- Attack fails → keep the test as a regression; record your attempt inventory
  as analysis evidence:
  `security-check <ID> --analysis attack-notes.md --author agent:<you>`.
- Your notes must list ATTEMPTED sequences, not just conclusions. "No exploit
  found" without the attempt inventory is not evidence of anything.
- Never weaken an attack so it fails. Record the strongest version you can
  build.
